<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Union Defence Force (South Africa)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1912">1912</a>–<a href="/years/1957">1957</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Union Defence Force was the military of the Union of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>, established by the Defence Act of 1912. It fought in both world wars, conquering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-south-west-africa">German South West Africa</a> in 1915 and serving in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-africa">East Africa</a>, on the Western Front, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, and it was renamed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-african-defence-force">South African Defence Force</a> in 1957.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/union-defence-force-south-africa.jpg" alt="Union Defence Force (South Africa)" width="320" height="425" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Vectorebus · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Union_Defence_Force_emblem.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>After the Union of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> was formed in 1910, General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jan-smuts">Jan Smuts</a>, its first Minister of Defence, made it a priority to build one military out of the separate armies of the four provinces. The Defence Act of 1912, in force from 1 July, created a Permanent Force of career soldiers, an Active Citizen Force of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conscription">conscripts</a>, a Coast Garrison Force and a naval volunteer reserve. All white men from seventeen to sixty were liable to serve, but as volunteers were many, half of those aged 17 to 25 were drafted into the Citizen Force by lot.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Headquarters opened in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pretoria">Pretoria</a> on 1 July 1912. In 1913 and 1914 the Citizen Force was used to put down industrial strikes on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/witwatersrand">Witwatersrand</a>. The South African Air Force followed in 1920 and the Naval Service in 1922. Savings from 1926 and the Great Depression brought the disbanding of 56 Citizen Force units and of the Naval Service. Oswald Pirow, Minister of Defence from 1933, planned an expansion that had not been carried out by the start of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> the UDF took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-south-west-africa">German South West Africa</a>, sent an expedition under Smuts against Paul Erich von Lettow-Vorbeck in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-east-africa">German East Africa</a> and an infantry brigade to the Western Front, where Delville Wood in 1916 was its costliest battle. Over 250,000 South Africans of all races served and over 7,000 were killed.</p><p>In the Second World War South African forces fought the Italian forces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini">Benito Mussolini</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-africa">East Africa</a> in 1940 and 1941, took part in the invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madagascar">Madagascar</a> in 1942 and the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-alamein">El Alamein</a>, lost two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brigade">brigades</a> of the 2nd Infantry Division at the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tobruk">Tobruk</a> on 21 June 1942, and fought in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in 1944 and 1945. Of 334,000 men who volunteered for full-time service in the army, nearly 9,000 were killed in action.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The UDF also took part in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin-airlift">Berlin Airlift</a>, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> between 1950 and 1953 the air force's 2 Squadron, the Flying Cheetahs, served with the United Nations Command; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> lost 34 dead or missing there. In 1957 the force was reorganised and renamed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-african-defence-force">South African Defence Force</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Union Defence Force (South Africa)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
